    {
      "key": {
        "uuid": "18473e56_209f878b",
        "filename": "driver/accelgyro_bmi160.c",
        "patchSetId": 7
      },
      "lineNbr": 894,
      "author": {
        "id": 1186109
      },
      "writtenOn": "2017-07-06T23:37:39Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "Agreed.  While the rotation matrix can be used to determine sensor mounting position, at least one additional piece of information is needed to know how to translate orientation, that being whether the device, when in its default orientation, is in Portrait or Landscape mode (chromebooks will be in landscape mode when Y is up, devices like scarlet will be in portrait mode when Y is up).  Since we can\u0027t use rotation matrix conveniently to determine new orientation, it would be less processor-intensive if devices that want to use orientation and need to do orientation translation define a rotate_orientation() function inside their board.c file.  That would allow the orientation translation to be a simple case statement.  For scarlet (which has a BMI160), I had used a define (ORIENTATION_REMAP_X_Y_AXES) to indicate whether coordinate system needed rotation (patch in a different branch, I had not brought that change over to this patch yet), but prefer the idea of letting the board define the routine to avoid extra code.\n\nAre you ok with boards that need to do orientation translation do it via adding a rotate_orientation() function to their board.c file and defining ORIENTATION_REMAP_X_Y_AXES to indicate we need to call rotate_orientation()?  This approach would also avoid having to add a flag to indicate whether PORTRAIT or LANDSCAPE mode is the default device position.",
      "parentUuid": "c18c0257_6ab63c20",
      "revId": "e4ec17bbc55885f0ffac14503cf1409ace850dc9",
      "serverId": "3ce6091f-6c88-37e8-8c75-72f92ae8dfba",
      "unresolved": false
    },
    {
      "key": {
        "uuid": "0580dd3a_950d8d2a",
        "filename": "driver/accelgyro_bmi160.c",
        "patchSetId": 7
      },
      "lineNbr": 894,
      "author": {
        "id": 1002053
      },
      "writtenOn": "2017-07-07T18:35:40Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "It goes beyond just remamping X and Y axis: if the sensor is \"upside down\", we will end up reporting MOTIONSENSE_ORIENTATION_UPSIDE_DOWN_LANDSCAPE when MOTIONSENSE_ORIENTATION_LANDSCAPE is expected. \nSimilar problem when the sensor is solder on the other side of the PCB: when moving from LANDSCAPE to PORTRAIT, the sense will report LANDSCAPE to PORTRAIT_INVERTED.",
      "parentUuid": "18473e56_209f878b",
      "revId": "e4ec17bbc55885f0ffac14503cf1409ace850dc9",
      "serverId": "3ce6091f-6c88-37e8-8c75-72f92ae8dfba",
      "unresolved": false
    },
